  "root_cause": {
    "hypothesis": "Deploy of revision 44 (image bump python:3.12-alpine -> python:3.12.7-alpine) at 2026-09-11T17:51:50Z broke checkout's startup config validation. Every new task ECS spins up since then crashes immediately with 'fatal: configuration invalid' (6 distinct new task IDs, all fail within seconds of start), so ECS keeps cycling replacement tasks while the 2 pre-deploy tasks (859f784b, 2831b443) keep heartbeating fine on the old image. Net effect: deployment stuck failing, tasks_pending=1, alarm fires — old tasks hold the service up but new rollout can't complete. Likely cause: alpine base image minor-version bump changed installed package versions (openssl/musl/python patch) breaking config parsing/env loading at boot, or a config file baked into the new image tag is malformed/missing.",
    "confidence": 0.82,
    "evidence": [
      "Deploy registered_at 2026-09-11T17:51:49.997Z / at 17:51:50.220Z, ~40s before first 'fatal: configuration invalid' log at 17:52:31.732Z",
      "6 distinct new task IDs (861368c9, 2336e2a3, c3c98f5a, 03870fbc, 12a927a4, c7e192fc) each log the fatal error once then vanish, no heartbeat — classic crash-on-boot loop",
      "Old task IDs 859f784b and 2831b443 (pre-deploy) keep heartbeating every 15s uninterrupted through the whole window",
      "METRICS show deployments_in_flight=2 (old steady-state + new failing rollout), tasks_pending=1 (stuck replacement task), tasks_running=tasks_desired=2 (old tasks still covering)",
      "previous_image was python:3.12-alpine (floating minor), new is pinned 3.12.7-alpine — a real image content change, not a no-op redeploy"
    ],
    "ruled_out": [
      "Resource exhaustion (OOM/CPU starvation) — cpu ~0.005% and memory ~59% are unremarkable, and failing tasks die too fast for resource pressure to be the trigger",
      "Infra-wide/platform outage — old tasks on same service keep running clean, so failure is scoped to the new image/config, not ECS/cluster-level",
      "deployments_failed=0.0 suggests ECS hasn't yet marked the deployment failed/rolled back — this is an active, ongoing crash loop, not a completed failed deploy"
    ]
  },
